window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2018-12-1", "source": "2018年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on how to balance work and leisure. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "In today's fast-paced society, many people are so absorbed in their careers that they leave almost no room for\nrest. Yet balancing work and leisure is essential, for it protects our health and, in the long run, keeps us\nproductive. The real question is not why we should do it, but how. In my view, a sensible balance can be\nachieved through a few practical steps.\nFirst, we should plan our time reasonably. By drawing up a weekly schedule that fixes clear hours for work and\nfor relaxation, we avoid letting one swallow the other. Second, it is advisable to set firm boundaries. For\ninstance, switching off work emails after nine o'clock stops the office from invading our private life. Moreover,\ncultivating a hobby, such as running or painting, gives our mind a genuine break rather than an empty one.\nBy taking these steps, we can enjoy our jobs without being enslaved by them. Only when work and leisure are\nkept in harmony can we lead a healthy and fulfilling life."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，中国越来越多的博物馆免费向公众开放。博物馆展览次数和参观人数都明显增长。在一些广受欢迎的博物馆门前，排长队已很常见。这些博物馆必须采取措施限制参观人数。如今，展览形式越来越多样。一些大型博物馆利用多媒体和虚拟现实等先进技术，使展览更具吸引力。不少博物馆还举办在线展览，人们可在网上观赏珍稀展品。然而，现场观看展品的体验对大多数参观者还是更具吸引力。", "sample": "In recent years, an increasing number of museums in China have been open to the public free of charge. The number of exhibitions and museum visitors has increased significantly. In front of some popular museums, it is common to see visitors waiting in long lines. As a result, these museums have to take measures to limit the number of visitors. Today, the forms of exhibitions are becoming increasingly diverse. Some large museums employ advanced technologies such as multimedia and virtual reality (VR) to make their exhibitions more attractive. Many museums also hold online exhibitions, where people can appreciate rare exhibits on the Internet. However, for most visitors, the experience of viewing exhibits in person is still more attractive."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2018-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Just off the coast of Southern California sits Santa Cruz Island, where a magical creature called the island fox  __26__  . A decade ago, this island's ecosystem was in  __27__  . Wild pigs attracted golden eagles from the mainland, and those flying  __28__  crashed the fox population. So the Nature Conservancy launched a  __29__  war against the pigs, complete with helicopters and sharp shooters.\nAnd it worked. Today, federal agencies are pulling the island fox from the Endangered Species List. It's the fastest-ever recovery of a mammal, joining peers like the Louisiana black bear as glowing successes in the history of the Endangered Species Act.\nBut the recovery of Santa Cruz Island isn't just about the fox. The Nature Conservancy has  __30__  war on a multitude of invasive species here, from sheep to plants to the  __31__  Argentine ant. \"Our philosophy with the island has always been, 'OK,  __32__  the threats and let the island go back to what it was,'\" says ecologist Christina Boser. And it appears to be working. Native plants are coming back, and the fox once again bounds about carefree.\nBut keeping those foxes from harm will occupy Boser and her colleagues for years to come. You see, humans are still allowed on Santa Cruz Island, and they bring dogs. So Boser has to vaccinate her foxes against various diseases. \"We're obligated to keep a pulse on the population for at least five years after the foxes are delisted,\" says Boser. That includes tagging the foxes and  __33__  their numbers to ensure nothing goes wrong.\nThis is the story of the little fox that has come back, and the people who have  __34__  their lives to protecting it. This is the story of wildlife conservation in the age of mass  __35__  .", "matchIntro": "Do Parents Invade Children's Privacy When They Post Photos Online?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "When Katlyn Burbidge's son was 6 years old, he was performing some ridiculous song and dance typical of a first-grader. But after she snapped a photo and started using her phone, he asked her a serious question: \"Are you going to post that online?\" She laughed and answered, \"Yes, I think I will.\" What he said next stopped her. \"Can you not?\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "That's when it dawned on her: She had been posting photos of him online without asking his permission. \"We're big advocates of bodily autonomy and not forcing him to hug or kiss people unless he wants to, but it never occurred to me that I should ask his permission to post photos of him online,\" says Burbidge, a mom of two in Wakefield, Massachusetts. \"Now when I post a photo of him online, I show him the photo and get his okay.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "When her 8-month-old is 3 or 4 years old, she plans to start asking him in an age-appropriate way, \"Do you want other people to see this?\" That's precisely the approach that two researchers advocated before a room of pediatricians(儿科医生) last week at the American Academy of Pediatrics meeting, when they discussed the 21st century challenge of \"sharenting,\" a new term for parents' online sharing about their children. \"As advocates of children's rights, we believe that children should have a voice about what information is shared about them if possible,\" says Stacey Steinberg, a legal skills professor at the University of Florida Levin College of Law in Gainesville."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Whether it's ensuring that your child isn't bullied over something you post, that their identity isn't digitally \"kidnapped\", or that their photos don't end up on a half dozen child pornography(色情) sites, as one Australian mom discovered, parents and pediatricians are increasingly aware of the importance of protecting children's digital presence. Steinberg and Bahareh Keith, an assistant professor of pediatrics at the University of Florida College of Medicine, say most children will likely never experience problems related to what their parents share, but a tension still exists between parents' rights to share their experiences and their children's rights to privacy."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"We're in no way trying to silence parents' voices,\" Steinberg says. \"At the same time, we recognize that children might have an interest in entering adulthood free to create their own digital footprint.\" They cited a study presented earlier this year of 249 pairs of parents and their children in which twice as many children as parents wanted rules on what parents could share. \"The parents said, 'We don't need rules—we're fine,' and the children said, 'Our parents need rules,'\" Keith says. \"The children wanted autonomy about this issue and were worried about their parents sharing information about them.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Although the American Academy of Pediatrics offers guidelines recommending that parents model appropriate social media use for their children, it does not explicitly discuss oversharing by parents. \"I think this is a very legitimate concern, and I appreciate their drawing our attention to it,\" David Hill, a father of five, says. He sees a role for pediatricians to talk with parents about this, but believes the messaging must extend far beyond pediatricians' offices. \"I look forward to seeing researchers expand our understanding of the issue so we can translate it into effective education and policy,\" he says."}, {"label": "G", "text": "There's been little research on the topic, Steinberg wrote in a law article about this issue. While states could pass laws related to sharing information about children online, Steinberg feels parents themselves are generally best suited to make these decisions for their families. \"While we didn't want to create any unnecessary panic, we did find some concerns that were troublesome, and we thought that parents or at least physicians should be aware of those potential risks,\" Steinberg says. They include photos repurposed for inappropriate or illegal means, identity theft, embarrassment, bullying by peers or digital kidnapping."}, {"label": "H", "text": "But that's the negative side, with risks that must be balanced against the benefits of sharing. Steinberg pointed out that parental sharing on social media helps build communities, connect spread-out families, provide support and raise awareness around important social issues for which parents might be their children's only voice."}, {"label": "I", "text": "A C.S. Mott survey found among the 56 percent of mothers and 34 percent of fathers who discussed parenting on social media, 72 percent of them said sharing made them feel less alone, and nearly as many said sharing helped them worry less and gave them advice from other parents. The most common topics they discussed included kids' sleep, nutrition, discipline, behavior problems and day care and preschool."}, {"label": "J", "text": "\"There's this peer-to-peer nature of health care these days with a profound opportunity for parents to learn helpful tips, safety and prevention efforts, pro-vaccine messages and all kinds of other messages from other parents in their social communities,\" says Wendy Sue Swanson, a pediatrician and executive director of digital health at Seattle Children's Hospital, where she blogs about her own parenting journey to help other parents. \"They're getting nurtured by people they've already selected that they trust,\" she says."}, {"label": "K", "text": "\"How do we weigh the risks, how do we think about the benefits, and how do we alleviate the risks?\" she says. \"Those are the questions we need to ask ourselves, and everyone can have a different answer.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Some parents find the best route for them is not to share at all. Bridget O'Hanlon and her husband, who live in Cleveland, decided before their daughter was born that they would not post her photos online. When a few family members did post pictures, O'Hanlon and her husband made their wishes clear. \"It's been hard not to share pictures of her because people always want to know how babies and toddlers(学走路的孩子) are doing and to see pictures, but we made the decision to have social media while she did not,\" O'Hanlon said. Similarly, Alison Jamison of New York decided with her husband that their child had a right to their own online identity. They did use an invitation-only photo sharing platform so that friends and family, including those far away, could see the photos, but they stood firm, simply refusing to put their child's photos on other social media platforms."}, {"label": "M", "text": "\"For most families, it's a journey. Sometimes it goes wrong, but most of the time it doesn't,\" says Swanson, who recommends starting to ask children permission to post narratives or photos around ages 6 to 8. \"We'll learn more and more what our tolerance is. We can ask our kids to help us learn as a society what's okay and what's not.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "Indeed, that learning process goes both ways. Bria Dunham, a mother in Somerville, Massachusetts, was so excited to watch a moment of brotherly bonding while her first-grader and baby took a bath together that she snapped a few photos. But when she considered posting them online, she took the perspective of her son: How would he feel if his classmates' parents saw photos of him chest-up in the bathtub? \"It made me think about how I'm teaching him to have ownership of his own body and how what is shared today endures into the future,\" Dunham says. \"So I kept the pictures to myself and accepted this as one more step in supporting his increasing autonomy.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig up weeds, and even have become shepherds, too. Commercial growing fields are astronomically huge and take thousands of man-hours to operate. One prime example is one of Australia's most isolated cattle stations, Suplejack Downs in the Northern Territory, extending across 4,000 square kilometers, taking over 13 hours to reach by car from the nearest major town—Alice Springs.\nThe extreme isolation of these massive farms leaves them often unattended, and monitored only once or twice a year, which means if the livestock falls ill or requires assistance, it can be a long time for farmers to discover.\nHowever, robots are coming to the rescue.\nRobots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on. The robots are equipped with many sensors to identify conditions of the environment, cattle and food, using thermal and vision sensors that detect changes in body temperature.\n\"You've also got color, texture and shape sensors looking down at the ground to check pasture quality,\" says Salah Sukkarieh of the University of Sydney, who will carry out trials on several farms in central New South Wales.\nDuring the trials, the robot algorithms(算法) and mechanics will be fine-tuned to make it better suited to ailing livestock and ensure it safely navigates around potential hazards including trees, mud, swamps, and hills.\n\"We want to improve the quality of animal health and make it easier for farmers to maintain large landscapes where animals roam free,\" says Sukkarieh.\nThe robots are not limited to herding and monitoring livestock. They have been created to count individual fruit, inspect crops, and even pull weeds.\nMany robots are equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms to avoid injuring humans as they work side by side. The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers to analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\nOf course, some worry lies in replacing agricultural workers. However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor vacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\nThe robots have provided major benefits to farmers in various ways, from hunting and pulling weeds to monitoring the condition of every single fruit. Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and more farm work efficiently.", "47": "Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig up weeds, and even have become shepherds, too. Commercial growing fields are astronomically huge and take thousands of man-hours to operate. One prime example is one of Australia's most isolated cattle stations, Suplejack Downs in the Northern Territory, extending across 4,000 square kilometers, taking over 13 hours to reach by car from the nearest major town—Alice Springs.\nThe extreme isolation of these massive farms leaves them often unattended, and monitored only once or twice a year, which means if the livestock falls ill or requires assistance, it can be a long time for farmers to discover.\nHowever, robots are coming to the rescue.\nRobots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on. The robots are equipped with many sensors to identify conditions of the environment, cattle and food, using thermal and vision sensors that detect changes in body temperature.\n\"You've also got color, texture and shape sensors looking down at the ground to check pasture quality,\" says Salah Sukkarieh of the University of Sydney, who will carry out trials on several farms in central New South Wales.\nDuring the trials, the robot algorithms(算法) and mechanics will be fine-tuned to make it better suited to ailing livestock and ensure it safely navigates around potential hazards including trees, mud, swamps, and hills.\n\"We want to improve the quality of animal health and make it easier for farmers to maintain large landscapes where animals roam free,\" says Sukkarieh.\nThe robots are not limited to herding and monitoring livestock. They have been created to count individual fruit, inspect crops, and even pull weeds.\nMany robots are equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms to avoid injuring humans as they work side by side. The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers to analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\nOf course, some worry lies in replacing agricultural workers. However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor vacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\nThe robots have provided major benefits to farmers in various ways, from hunting and pulling weeds to monitoring the condition of every single fruit. Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and more farm work efficiently.", "48": "Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig up weeds, and even have become shepherds, too. Commercial growing fields are astronomically huge and take thousands of man-hours to operate. One prime example is one of Australia's most isolated cattle stations, Suplejack Downs in the Northern Territory, extending across 4,000 square kilometers, taking over 13 hours to reach by car from the nearest major town—Alice Springs.\nThe extreme isolation of these massive farms leaves them often unattended, and monitored only once or twice a year, which means if the livestock falls ill or requires assistance, it can be a long time for farmers to discover.\nHowever, robots are coming to the rescue.\nRobots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on. The robots are equipped with many sensors to identify conditions of the environment, cattle and food, using thermal and vision sensors that detect changes in body temperature.\n\"You've also got color, texture and shape sensors looking down at the ground to check pasture quality,\" says Salah Sukkarieh of the University of Sydney, who will carry out trials on several farms in central New South Wales.\nDuring the trials, the robot algorithms(算法) and mechanics will be fine-tuned to make it better suited to ailing livestock and ensure it safely navigates around potential hazards including trees, mud, swamps, and hills.\n\"We want to improve the quality of animal health and make it easier for farmers to maintain large landscapes where animals roam free,\" says Sukkarieh.\nThe robots are not limited to herding and monitoring livestock. They have been created to count individual fruit, inspect crops, and even pull weeds.\nMany robots are equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms to avoid injuring humans as they work side by side. The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers to analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\nOf course, some worry lies in replacing agricultural workers. However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor vacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\nThe robots have provided major benefits to farmers in various ways, from hunting and pulling weeds to monitoring the condition of every single fruit. Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and more farm work efficiently.", "49": "Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig up weeds, and even have become shepherds, too. Commercial growing fields are astronomically huge and take thousands of man-hours to operate. One prime example is one of Australia's most isolated cattle stations, Suplejack Downs in the Northern Territory, extending across 4,000 square kilometers, taking over 13 hours to reach by car from the nearest major town—Alice Springs.\nThe extreme isolation of these massive farms leaves them often unattended, and monitored only once or twice a year, which means if the livestock falls ill or requires assistance, it can be a long time for farmers to discover.\nHowever, robots are coming to the rescue.\nRobots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on. The robots are equipped with many sensors to identify conditions of the environment, cattle and food, using thermal and vision sensors that detect changes in body temperature.\n\"You've also got color, texture and shape sensors looking down at the ground to check pasture quality,\" says Salah Sukkarieh of the University of Sydney, who will carry out trials on several farms in central New South Wales.\nDuring the trials, the robot algorithms(算法) and mechanics will be fine-tuned to make it better suited to ailing livestock and ensure it safely navigates around potential hazards including trees, mud, swamps, and hills.\n\"We want to improve the quality of animal health and make it easier for farmers to maintain large landscapes where animals roam free,\" says Sukkarieh.\nThe robots are not limited to herding and monitoring livestock. They have been created to count individual fruit, inspect crops, and even pull weeds.\nMany robots are equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms to avoid injuring humans as they work side by side. The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers to analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\nOf course, some worry lies in replacing agricultural workers. However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor vacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\nThe robots have provided major benefits to farmers in various ways, from hunting and pulling weeds to monitoring the condition of every single fruit. Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and more farm work efficiently.", "50": "Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig up weeds, and even have become shepherds, too. Commercial growing fields are astronomically huge and take thousands of man-hours to operate. One prime example is one of Australia's most isolated cattle stations, Suplejack Downs in the Northern Territory, extending across 4,000 square kilometers, taking over 13 hours to reach by car from the nearest major town—Alice Springs.\nThe extreme isolation of these massive farms leaves them often unattended, and monitored only once or twice a year, which means if the livestock falls ill or requires assistance, it can be a long time for farmers to discover.\nHowever, robots are coming to the rescue.\nRobots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on. The robots are equipped with many sensors to identify conditions of the environment, cattle and food, using thermal and vision sensors that detect changes in body temperature.\n\"You've also got color, texture and shape sensors looking down at the ground to check pasture quality,\" says Salah Sukkarieh of the University of Sydney, who will carry out trials on several farms in central New South Wales.\nDuring the trials, the robot algorithms(算法) and mechanics will be fine-tuned to make it better suited to ailing livestock and ensure it safely navigates around potential hazards including trees, mud, swamps, and hills.\n\"We want to improve the quality of animal health and make it easier for farmers to maintain large landscapes where animals roam free,\" says Sukkarieh.\nThe robots are not limited to herding and monitoring livestock. They have been created to count individual fruit, inspect crops, and even pull weeds.\nMany robots are equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms to avoid injuring humans as they work side by side. The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers to analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\nOf course, some worry lies in replacing agricultural workers. However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor vacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\nThe robots have provided major benefits to farmers in various ways, from hunting and pulling weeds to monitoring the condition of every single fruit. Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and more farm work efficiently.", "51": "The public must be able to understand the basics of science to make informed decisions. Perhaps the most dramatic example of the negative consequences of poor communication between scientists and the public is the issue of climate change, where a variety of factors, not the least of which is a breakdown in the transmission of fundamental climate data to the general public, has contributed to widespread mistrust and misunderstanding of scientists and their research.\nThe issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of science (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to regulation, science policy and research funding.\nHowever, the importance of effective communication with a general audience is not limited to hot issues like climate change. It is also critical for socially charged neuroscience issues such as the genetic basis for a particular behavior, the therapeutic potential of stem cell therapy for neurodegenerative diseases, or the use of animal models, areas where the public understanding of science can also influence policy and funding decisions. Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the advent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing complex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\nScience journalism is the main channel for the popularization of scientific information among the public. Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape the efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public. Good science journalists are specialists in making complex topics accessible to a general audience, while adhering to scientific accuracy.\nUnfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their subject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong. The impact of a basic discovery on human health can be exaggerated so that the public thinks a miraculous cure is a few months to years away when in reality the significance of the study is far more limited.\nEven though scientists play a part in transmitting information to journalists and ultimately the public, too often the blame for ineffective communication is placed on the side of the journalists. We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between scientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for another most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication.", "52": "The public must be able to understand the basics of science to make informed decisions. Perhaps the most dramatic example of the negative consequences of poor communication between scientists and the public is the issue of climate change, where a variety of factors, not the least of which is a breakdown in the transmission of fundamental climate data to the general public, has contributed to widespread mistrust and misunderstanding of scientists and their research.\nThe issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of science (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to regulation, science policy and research funding.\nHowever, the importance of effective communication with a general audience is not limited to hot issues like climate change. It is also critical for socially charged neuroscience issues such as the genetic basis for a particular behavior, the therapeutic potential of stem cell therapy for neurodegenerative diseases, or the use of animal models, areas where the public understanding of science can also influence policy and funding decisions. Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the advent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing complex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\nScience journalism is the main channel for the popularization of scientific information among the public. Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape the efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public. Good science journalists are specialists in making complex topics accessible to a general audience, while adhering to scientific accuracy.\nUnfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their subject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong. The impact of a basic discovery on human health can be exaggerated so that the public thinks a miraculous cure is a few months to years away when in reality the significance of the study is far more limited.\nEven though scientists play a part in transmitting information to journalists and ultimately the public, too often the blame for ineffective communication is placed on the side of the journalists. We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between scientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for another most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication.", "53": "The public must be able to understand the basics of science to make informed decisions. Perhaps the most dramatic example of the negative consequences of poor communication between scientists and the public is the issue of climate change, where a variety of factors, not the least of which is a breakdown in the transmission of fundamental climate data to the general public, has contributed to widespread mistrust and misunderstanding of scientists and their research.\nThe issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of science (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to regulation, science policy and research funding.\nHowever, the importance of effective communication with a general audience is not limited to hot issues like climate change. It is also critical for socially charged neuroscience issues such as the genetic basis for a particular behavior, the therapeutic potential of stem cell therapy for neurodegenerative diseases, or the use of animal models, areas where the public understanding of science can also influence policy and funding decisions. Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the advent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing complex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\nScience journalism is the main channel for the popularization of scientific information among the public. Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape the efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public. Good science journalists are specialists in making complex topics accessible to a general audience, while adhering to scientific accuracy.\nUnfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their subject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong. The impact of a basic discovery on human health can be exaggerated so that the public thinks a miraculous cure is a few months to years away when in reality the significance of the study is far more limited.\nEven though scientists play a part in transmitting information to journalists and ultimately the public, too often the blame for ineffective communication is placed on the side of the journalists. We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between scientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for another most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication.", "54": "The public must be able to understand the basics of science to make informed decisions. Perhaps the most dramatic example of the negative consequences of poor communication between scientists and the public is the issue of climate change, where a variety of factors, not the least of which is a breakdown in the transmission of fundamental climate data to the general public, has contributed to widespread mistrust and misunderstanding of scientists and their research.\nThe issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of science (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to regulation, science policy and research funding.\nHowever, the importance of effective communication with a general audience is not limited to hot issues like climate change. It is also critical for socially charged neuroscience issues such as the genetic basis for a particular behavior, the therapeutic potential of stem cell therapy for neurodegenerative diseases, or the use of animal models, areas where the public understanding of science can also influence policy and funding decisions. Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the advent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing complex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\nScience journalism is the main channel for the popularization of scientific information among the public. Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape the efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public. Good science journalists are specialists in making complex topics accessible to a general audience, while adhering to scientific accuracy.\nUnfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their subject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong. The impact of a basic discovery on human health can be exaggerated so that the public thinks a miraculous cure is a few months to years away when in reality the significance of the study is far more limited.\nEven though scientists play a part in transmitting information to journalists and ultimately the public, too often the blame for ineffective communication is placed on the side of the journalists. We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between scientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for another most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication.", "55": "The public must be able to understand the basics of science to make informed decisions. Perhaps the most dramatic example of the negative consequences of poor communication between scientists and the public is the issue of climate change, where a variety of factors, not the least of which is a breakdown in the transmission of fundamental climate data to the general public, has contributed to widespread mistrust and misunderstanding of scientists and their research.\nThe issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of science (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to regulation, science policy and research funding.\nHowever, the importance of effective communication with a general audience is not limited to hot issues like climate change. It is also critical for socially charged neuroscience issues such as the genetic basis for a particular behavior, the therapeutic potential of stem cell therapy for neurodegenerative diseases, or the use of animal models, areas where the public understanding of science can also influence policy and funding decisions. Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the advent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing complex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\nScience journalism is the main channel for the popularization of scientific information among the public. Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape the efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public. Good science journalists are specialists in making complex topics accessible to a general audience, while adhering to scientific accuracy.\nUnfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their subject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong. The impact of a basic discovery on human health can be exaggerated so that the public thinks a miraculous cure is a few months to years away when in reality the significance of the study is far more limited.\nEven though scientists play a part in transmitting information to journalists and ultimately the public, too often the blame for ineffective communication is placed on the side of the journalists. We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between scientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for another most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication."}, "questions": [{"id": "2018-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the woman say about the book the man recommended?", "options": ["A) It can benefit professionals and non-professionals alike.", "B) It lists the various challenges physicists are confronting.", "C) It describes how some mysteries of physics were solved.", "D) It is one of the most fascinating physics books ever written."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It can benefit professionals and non-professionals alike.\n【定位】I think it promises enrichment for any reader, from those who know little about science to the\ncareer physicist.\n我认为它能让任何读者都有收获，从对科学知之甚少的人，到职业物理学家。\n【信号】被问者女士的直接评价句。本题问 the woman 的看法，定位就锁女士的话。她说 it\npromises enrichment for any reader, from those who know little about science to\nthe career physicist，这句「面向任何读者」的概括评价正是答案句。\n【替换】from those who know little about science to the career physicist ↔\nprofessionals and non-professionals alike\n「外行到职业」概括为「专业与非专业都」\nit promises enrichment for any reader ↔ It can benefit\npromises enrichment = benefit\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB说书「列出物理学家面临的种种挑战」，借了 physicists 这个全篇高频词诱你选，但录音\n里女士说的是适合各类读者，从未说本书在「罗列挑战」，属答非所问。\n借用原文：the career physicist\nC) 原词陷阱\nC说书「描述了某些物理之谜是如何被解开的」，盗用了女士口中的 mysteries 一词，但她\n只说作者是引领读者了解物理之谜的「向导」，没说书在讲「谜题被解开的过程」，偷换了\n概念。\n借用原文：the mysteries of physics\nD) 过度推断\nD说它是「有史以来最引人入胜的物理书之一」，女士确实夸了书（great），但绝没说「史\n上最…之一」这种最高级定位，属过度推断、把一般称赞拔成顶级评价。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What can we find in the book the man recommended?", "options": ["A) Physicists' contribution to humanity.", "B) Stories about some female physicists.", "C) Historical evolution of modern physics.", "D) Women's changing attitudes to physics."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Stories about some female physicists.\n【定位】I think she is to be commended for making an effort to include anecdotes about little-known\nfemale scientists.\n我认为她值得称赞，因为她努力收入了关于鲜为人知的女科学家的轶事。\n【信号】明示「书中收入了…」的内容句。男士说 making an effort to include anecdotes about\nlittle-known female scientists，include（收入、包含）直接回应题干「书里能找到什\n么」，是承载答案的内容句。\n【替换】anecdotes about little-known female scientists ↔ Stories about some female physicists\nanecdotes=Stories，scientists≈physicists\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA说「物理学家对人类的贡献」，听着很正能量、像物理书该有的内容，但录音通篇没说书\n在讲谁对人类有何贡献，属未提及的想当然。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC说「现代物理的历史演变」，借了贯穿全篇的 physics 话题词，但录音从没讲物理学如何\n一步步演变的历史线，属未提及。\n借用原文：the mysteries of physics\nD) 偷换概念\nD说「女性对物理态度的转变」，盗用了 P3 里 the woman's place 这类涉及女性的词，但\n录音说的是收入女科学家的故事，而非讲女性「态度如何变化」，张冠李戴、偷换了主题。\n借用原文：the woman's place was in the home", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "How does the author bring her book to life?", "options": ["A) By exposing a lot of myths in physics.", "B) By describing her own life experiences.", "C) By including lots of fascinating knowledge.", "D) By telling anecdotes about famous professors."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） By including lots of fascinating knowledge.\n【定位】I like how the book is clearly written with each chapter brought to life by pieces of\nfascinating knowledge.\n我喜欢这本书写得清晰，每一章都被一个个引人入胜的知识点带得活灵活现。\n【信号】题干原话 brought to life 的对应句。题干问 bring her book to life，录音几乎原话出现\neach chapter brought to life by pieces of fascinating knowledge，by 引出的正是手\n段，是答案句。\n【替换】brought to life by pieces of fascinating knowledge ↔ By including lots of fascinating knowledge\nby…fascinating knowledge=By including…fascinating knowledge\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA说「靠揭穿物理学中的大量迷思」让书生动，盗用了 P4 里 a myth（迷思）一词，但录音\n里「揭穿迷思」只是举的一个例子，并非女士点明的「让书生动」的总手段；且原文是 a\nmyth（单个）而非 a lot of myths（大量），张冠李戴又夸大了数量。\n借用原文：she exposes a myth that I've heard taught by university physics professors\nB) 未提及\nB说「靠描述她自己的人生经历」，听着像作者会用的招，但录音从没说书里写了作者的个\n人经历，属未提及。\nD) 张冠李戴\nD说「靠讲名教授的逸事」让书生动，盗用了 P4 里 university physics professors 一词，\n但原文是说「她揭穿了一个连大学物理教授都在教的迷思」，并非「讲教授的逸事」，把教\n授的角色彻底安错了。\n借用原文：I've heard taught by university physics professors", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "How does the book cultivate readers' interest in physics?", "options": ["A) It avoids detailing abstract concepts of physics.", "B) It contains a lot of thought-provoking questions.", "C) It demonstrates how they can become physicists.", "D) It provides experiments they can do themselves."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It provides experiments they can do themselves.\n【定位】I like how she cultivates scientific engagement by providing a host of do-it-yourself\nexperiments that bring the same foundational principles of classical physics.\n我喜欢她通过提供大量可自己动手做的实验来培养读者对科学的投入，这些实验呈现了经典\n物理同样的基础原理。\n【信号】题干同源词 cultivate + by 引出做法。录音 cultivates scientific engagement by\nproviding a host of do-it-yourself experiments 与题干 cultivate 同词，by 后引出的\n「提供可自己动手做的实验」正是答案句。\n【替换】do-it-yourself experiments ↔ experiments they can do themselves\ndo-it-yourself=they can do themselves\nhands-on examples make her book a truly interactive read ↔\nIt provides experiments they can do themselves.\nhands-on/interactive 印证「亲自做」\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA说书「避免详述物理的抽象概念」，看似呼应末句 equation-free（无公式），但「不含\n公式」不等于「回避讲抽象概念」，且这也不是录音回答「如何培养兴趣」的那句话，属过\n度推断。\n借用原文：this equation-free book is an ideal read\nB) 未提及\nB说书「含大量发人深省的问题」，是想当然的科普书套路，但录音说的是提供动手实验，\n从没说书里设有大量问题，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC说书「展示读者如何能成为物理学家」，盗用了全篇高频的 physicist 一词，但录音只说\n书让读者动手参与、产生兴趣，绝没说能教人「成为物理学家」，过度拔高了书的功能。\n借用原文：the career physicist", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is the man's problem?", "options": ["A) He is too busy to finish his assignment in time.", "B) He does not know what kind of topic to write on.", "C) He does not understand the professor's instructions.", "D) He has no idea how to proceed with his dissertation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has no idea how to proceed with his dissertation.\n【定位】I'm having some problems getting started on my dissertation, and I was hoping you could\ngive me some advice on how to begin.\n我在着手写毕业论文上遇到了点麻烦，希望您能给我点建议，告诉我该怎么起步。\n【信号】求助者开口自报处境。对话一开场，男生就用 I'm having some problems getting\nstarted on my dissertation 直接交代自己卡在哪——\"开口自报处境\"就是 problem 题的\n答案信号，不必等后文。\n【替换】getting started on my dissertation ↔ how to proceed with his dissertation\n起步=怎么往下推进\nI'm having some problems ↔ He has no idea\n有麻烦=没头绪\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项说他 too busy to finish his assignment in time（太忙赶不及），可男生从头到尾没\n说自己忙、也没提时间紧，他说的是不知怎么起步，属无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项说他 does not know what kind of topic to write on（不知写什么主题）。男生其实\n已选定主题（后文 aesthetics），问题是选好后\"不知怎么往下\"，不是没题目，这里借了后\n文反复出现的 topic 一词来误导。\n借用原文：The general topic I chose is aesthetics\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项说他 does not understand the professor's instructions。\"instructions\"确在录音里\n出现，但那是后文教授叮嘱 follow the instructions，跟男生当前\"不会起步\"的问题无关，\n是把别处的词搬来凑。\n借用原文：make sure to follow the instructions", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the professor think of the man's topic?", "options": ["A) It is too broad.", "B) It is a bit outdated.", "C) It is challenging.", "D) It is interesting."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is too broad.\n【定位】That's much too large a topic. You really need to narrow it down in order to make it\nmore accessible.\n这题目太大了。你真得把它缩小，才好驾驭。\n【信号】评价者直接下断语。教授应答时一句 That's much too large a topic 直接给出评价，紧接\n着 narrow it down（缩小范围）又印证\"太大\"——评价者直接下断语，就是态度题的答案\n信号。\n【替换】much too large a topic ↔ too broad\n太大=太宽泛\nnarrow it down ↔ too broad\n需缩小↔现太宽\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项说题目 a bit outdated（有点过时），但教授评的是\"太大\"，全程没提时间新旧，属无\n中生有。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项说 It is challenging.（有挑战）。教授嫌它\"太大需缩小\"，并非夸它有难度有价值，\nchallenging 把负面评价偷换成中性偏褒，方向不符。\n借用原文：much too large a topic\nD) 与原文相反\n选项说 It is interesting.（有趣）是正面评价，但教授给的是负面评价（太大），极性正好\n相反，是想当然往好处想的人会掉的坑。\n借用原文：much too large a topic", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What is the man really more interested in?", "options": ["A) Biography.", "B) Nature.", "C) Philosophy.", "D) Beauty."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Nature.\n【定位】I was hoping it would be possible for me to change topics. I'm really more interested in nature\nthan beauty.\n我本来还希望能换个题目。比起美，我其实更感兴趣的是自然。\n【信号】more...than 比较句点出取舍。男生说 I'm really more interested in nature than\nbeauty，一个 more...than 把两样东西摆上天平、明确偏向 nature——比较句就是\"更喜欢\n哪个\"的答案信号。\n【替换】more interested in nature ↔ Nature\n原句取 more 后那项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Biography 确在录音里出现，但那是教授让他去图书馆借的 get a copy of his\nbiography，是\"该读的材料\"，不是男生\"更感兴趣的东西\"，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：get a copy of his biography\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Philosophy 蹭了录音里 He's the philosopher 的 philosopher 一词，但那是在介绍\nHume 的身份，男生并没说更喜欢哲学，是借音近词设的坑。\n借用原文：He's the philosopher who wrote about where our ideas of beauty come from\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 Beauty 是比较句里 than 后面那一项——正是被比下去的一方。录音确实念了\nbeauty，但男生说的是更喜欢 nature 而非 beauty，取错了天平的另一端。\n借用原文：more interested in nature than beauty", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the professor say the man has to do?", "options": ["A) Improve his cumulative grade.", "B) Develop his reading ability.", "C) Stick to the topic assigned.", "D) List the parameters first."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Stick to the topic assigned.\n【定位】I'm afraid you have to adhere to the assigned topic.\n恐怕你得守着那个被指定的题目（不能换）。\n【信号】have to 引出硬性要求。教授一句 you have to adhere to the assigned topic，have to\n（必须）就是\"被要求做什么\"的答案信号，紧跟其后的动作即答案。\n【替换】adhere to the assigned topic ↔ Stick to the topic assigned\nadhere to=stick to\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Improve his cumulative grade. 蹭了录音里 This paper is a large part of your\ncumulative grade，但那只是解释\"为什么要照做\"的背景，不是教授要他做的动作，把背景\n目的偷换成任务。\n借用原文：This paper is a large part of your cumulative grade\nB) 未提及\n选项 Develop his reading ability. 录音根本没要求他提升阅读能力，他只是被叫去图书馆\n借本传记，属无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 List the parameters first. 蹭了录音里 stick to the parameters of the assignment\n的 parameters，但原文是\"守住作业的各项规定\"，不是\"先把参数列出来\"，把\"遵守\"偷换\n成\"列出\"。\n借用原文：remember to stick to the parameters of the assignment", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What did climate scientists describe as \"stunning\"?", "options": ["A) The unprecedented high temperature in Greenland.", "B) The collapse of ice on the northern tip of Greenland.", "C) The unusual cold spell in the Arctic area in October.", "D) The rapid change of Arctic temperature within a day."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The unprecedented high temperature in Greenland.\n【定位】On February 24, the temperature on Greenland's northern tip reached six degrees Celsius.\nClimate scientists described the phenomenon as \"stunning.\"\n2月24日，格陵兰北端的气温达到了6摄氏度。气候科学家把这一现象称作\"令人震惊的\"。\n【信号】stunning紧贴现象。答案信号是题干原词 described the phenomenon as \"stunning\"\n的出现位置——它紧跟在 the temperature on Greenland’s northern tip reached six\ndegrees Celsius 之后，the phenomenon 指代的正是这句反常高温，所以这句就是答\n案句。\n【替换】the temperature on Greenland’s northern tip reached six degrees Celsius ↔\nThe unprecedented high temperature in Greenland.\n具体数字概括为高温\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 的 collapse of ice 借用了全文反复出现的\"冰\"话题词来诱选，但原文 stunning 形容的\n是\"高温\"，从未说北端的冰\"崩塌\"，属听到相关词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：rapid sea-ice loss\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说 unusual cold spell（反常寒潮），与原文\"高温\"正好相反；原文开头虽提到 minus\n20 degrees 的常态低温，但被 stunning 形容的是升温而非降温。\n借用原文：the average temperature in the frozen north typically hovers around minus 20\ndegrees Celsius\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说\"一天之内\"温度急剧变化，原文 it stayed there for over 24 hours 说的是高温\"持\n续\"24小时以上，而非\"一天内骤变\"，盗用 24 hours 制造误导。\n借用原文：it stayed there for over 24 hours", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the passage say about the temperature surge in the Arctic?", "options": ["A) It has created a totally new climate pattern.", "B) It will pose a serious threat to many species.", "C) It typically appears about once every ten years.", "D) It has puzzled the climate scientists for decades."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It typically appears about once every ten years.\n【定位】Weather conditions that drive this bizarre temperature surge have visited the Arctic before.\nThey typically appear about once in a decade.\n驱动这种异常升温的天气状况此前也光顾过北极。它们通常大约每十年出现一次。\n【信号】题干原词surge加频率词。答案信号是题干同词 temperature surge 直接出现，紧跟其后的\nThey typically appear about once in a decade 给出频率，频率/数字句正是这类细节题\n的落点。\n【替换】once in a decade ↔ once every ten years\ndecade即ten years\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说\"创造了全新的气候模式\"，借用了原文 a new type of climate feedback loop 里\n的\"new/climate\"，但原文说的是\"新型反馈循环\"，且 A 的 totally new climate pattern 把\n范围夸成\"全新气候模式\"，说过头了。\n借用原文：a new type of climate feedback loop\nB) 答非所问\nB 说\"对许多物种构成严重威胁\"，原文 This raises unique challenges for Arctic wildlife\n确实提到对野生动物的挑战，但那是讲\"整体变暖\"的后果，不是回答 temperature\nsurge\"是什么特点\"，把别处的内容安到本题上。\n借用原文：This raises unique challenges for Arctic wildlife\nD) 未提及\nD 说\"困扰气候科学家数十年\"，原文只说科学家称该现象 stunning、并说它\"两年前刚发生\n过\"，从未说它\"困扰科学家数十年\"，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What may occur in 20 years according to scientists' recent observations?", "options": ["A) Extinction of Arctic wildlife.", "B) Iceless summers in the Arctic.", "C) Emigration of indigenous people.", "D) Better understanding of ecosystems."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Iceless summers in the Arctic.\n【定位】But based on what scientists are seeing now, the Arctic may be facing summers without ice\nwithin 20 years.\n但根据科学家如今所观察到的情况，北极可能在20年内就要面对没有冰的夏天了。\n【信号】转折but加时间预测。答案信号有两层：一是转折词 But based on what scientists are\nseeing now，转折后才是基于\"近期观察\"的新结论；二是题干同义时间 within 20 years，\n这句正是20年内的预测，即答案句。\n【替换】summers without ice ↔ Iceless summers in the Arctic.\nwithout ice即iceless\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 说\"北极野生动物灭绝\"，原文 This raises unique challenges for Arctic wildlife 只说\"给\n野生动物带来挑战\"，没说\"灭绝\"extinction，从\"挑战\"推到\"灭绝\"是过度推断。\n借用原文：This raises unique challenges for Arctic wildlife\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说\"原住民迁移\"，借用原文 indigenous people who depend on Arctic ecosystems to\nsurvive 里的\"原住民\"一词，但原文只说他们\"依赖北极生态系统生存\"，从未说他们要\"迁\n移\"emigration。\n借用原文：indigenous people who depend on Arctic ecosystems to survive\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说\"更好地理解生态系统\"，盗用原文 ecosystems 一词，但原文谈的是变暖带来的威胁与\n无冰预测，根本没说会\"更好理解生态系统\"，且这与全文消极基调相反。\n借用原文：indigenous people who depend on Arctic ecosystems to survive", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is often necessary for carrying through a task?", "options": ["A) A good start.", "B) A detailed plan.", "C) A strong determination.", "D) A scientific approach."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） A strong determination.\n【定位】A good dose of willpower is often necessary to see any task through, whether it's sticking to a\nspending plan or finishing a great novel.\n要把任何一件事做到底，往往都需要相当的意志力，无论是坚持一份花销计划，还是写完一\n部长篇小说。\n【信号】开头总起句+原词 necessary 重现。录音第一句 A good dose of willpower is often\nnecessary to see any task through 与题干 What is often necessary for carrying\nthrough a task? 几乎同形（necessary、task through 对 carrying through a task），这\n是篇章独白典型的『开头点题』，答案信号就是这句把核心要素 willpower 直接摆出来。\n【替换】A good dose of willpower ↔ A strong determination.\nwillpower=determination 近义替换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA good start. 借用了录音 A good dose of willpower 里的 good 来勾人，但录音说的是\na good dose（充足的量），与 a good start（好的开端）毫无关系，属听到 good 就想选\n的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：A good dose of willpower\nB) 原词陷阱\nA detailed plan. 的 plan 来自录音举例 sticking to a spending plan，那只是『做成事』\n的一个例子，并非录音点名的必要要素，把例子里的词当成答案即落入原词陷阱。\n借用原文：sticking to a spending plan\nD) 未提及\nA scientific approach. 录音从头到尾没说过完成任务需要『科学方法』，第一段讲的是\nwillpower，与 approach 无关，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What is the finding of the new study?", "options": ["A) Most people get energized after a sufficient rest.", "B) Most people tend to have a finite source of energy.", "C) It is vital to take breaks between demanding mental tasks.", "D) It is most important to have confidence in one's willpower."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is most important to have confidence in one's willpower.\n【定位】And if you want to increase that willpower, a new study suggests you just simply have\nto believe you have it. According to the study, what matters most is what we think about\nour willpower.\n如果你想增强意志力，一项新研究表明，你只需相信自己拥有它即可。根据这项研究，最重\n要的是我们如何看待自己的意志力。\n【信号】结论标志语 a new study suggests + what matters most。录音用 a new study\nsuggests you just simply have to believe you have it 和 what matters most is what\nwe think about our willpower 直接抛出研究结论，suggests、what matters most 都是\n结论信号词，紧跟其后的就是 finding。\n【替换】you just simply have to believe you have it ↔\nIt is most important to have confidence in one's willpower.\nbelieve you have it=have confidence in willpower\nwhat matters most is what we think about our willpower ↔\nIt is most important to have confidence in one's willpower.\nwhat matters most=It is most important\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMost people get energized after a sufficient rest. 盗用录音 get energized 与\nbreak/rest 概念，但录音说『把意志力看成无限的人才会 get energized』，且这并非研究\n的核心 finding，而是对『信念有限派』的描述，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：people who view their willpower as a limitless resource get energized\nB) 偷换概念\nMost people tend to have a finite source of energy. 直接搬录音 If we believe it's a\nfinite resource 里的 finite resource，但录音是说『若你相信它有限，你就会那样表现』，\n强调的是信念而非『精力客观有限』，更不是研究的最终 finding，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：If we believe it's a finite resource, we act that way\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt is vital to take breaks between demanding mental tasks. 借录音 need breaks\nbetween demanding mental tasks 的原词，但录音只是描述『信念有限的人会觉得需要\n休息』，并未主张『休息很关键 vital』，研究真正的结论是相信自己，属与原文相反/偷\n换。\n借用原文：need breaks between demanding mental tasks", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about European participants as compared with their American counterparts?", "options": ["A) They could keep on working longer.", "B) They could do more challenging tasks.", "C) They found it easier to focus on work at hand.", "D) They held more positive attitudes toward life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They could keep on working longer.\n【定位】Americans were more likely to admit to needing breaks after completing mentally\nchallenging tasks. European participants, on the other hand, claimed they were able to\nkeep going.\n美国人更倾向于承认在完成高难度脑力任务后需要休息。而欧洲受试者则声称他们能够坚持\n下去。\n【信号】对比信号 on the other hand 之后才是欧洲人。录音用 on the other hand 把美国人与欧\n洲人的表现一分为二，欧洲人的描述在转折后半句 claimed they were able to keep\ngoing，对比信号词 on the other hand 是定位答案句的钥匙。\n【替换】claimed they were able to keep going ↔ They could keep on working longer.\nable to keep going=could keep on working longer\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey could do more challenging tasks. 借录音 mentally challenging tasks 的\nchallenging，但录音讲的是欧洲人能『坚持更久』，从没说他们能做『更难的任务』，属\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：mentally challenging tasks\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey found it easier to focus on work at hand. 的 focus 来自录音 I can focus on a\nmental task for long periods，那是问卷里的陈述句举例，不是描述欧洲人相比美国人的\n差异，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I can focus on a mental task for long periods without feeling tired\nD) 未提及\nThey held more positive attitudes toward life. 录音根本没说欧洲人对『生活』更乐观，\npositive 一词出现在第三段讲信念的 positive effects，与欧洲人无关，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do the researchers say concerning people's feelings about willpower?", "options": ["A) They are part of their nature.", "B) They are subject to change.", "C) They are related to culture.", "D) They are beyond control."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are subject to change.\n【定位】\"Your feelings about your willpower affect the way you behave. But these feelings are\nchangeable,\" they said.\n他们说：『你对意志力的感受会影响你的行为方式。但这些感受是可以改变的。』\n【信号】转折 But 之后的定性词 changeable。录音研究者原话里 But these feelings are\nchangeable 用转折词 But 把重心拉到『可改变』上，changeable 是这句的定性落点，转\n折后才是真意，正是答案信号。\n【替换】these feelings are changeable ↔ They are subject to change.\nchangeable=subject to change\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are part of their nature. 借录音 Changing your beliefs about the nature of your\nself-control 的 nature，但录音讲的是『可以改变』，与『天生不可改的本性』正相反，属\n与原文相反。\n借用原文：But these feelings are changeable\nC) 未提及\nThey are related to culture. 录音从未说意志力的感受『与文化有关』，第二段虽比较了美\n欧受试者，但那是地域差异，研究者并没把感受归因于 culture，属未提及。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey are beyond control. 借录音 the nature of your self-control 的 control，但研究者\n说感受 changeable、能带来积极改变，恰恰是『可控可改』，与『beyond control 不可\n控』相反。\n借用原文：these feelings are changeable", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What did the researchers at Oxford University conclude?", "options": ["A) About half of current jobs might be automated.", "B) The jobs of doctors and lawyers would be threatened.", "C) The job market is becoming somewhat unpredictable.", "D) Machine learning would prove disruptive by 2013."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） About half of current jobs might be automated.\n【定位】They concluded that almost one in every two jobs has a high risk of being automated\nby machines.\n他们得出结论：几乎每两份工作里就有一份面临被机器自动化取代的高风险。\n【信号】结论动词 concluded 领出落点。原文 They concluded that 直接对应题干的 conclude，\n这种\"下结论\"的标志动词后面紧跟的就是答案句；听到 concluded/found/argued 这类\n词，后面那句基本就是考点。\n【替换】almost one in every two jobs ↔ About half of current jobs\n\"二分之一\"= about half\nhas a high risk of being automated by machines ↔ might be automated\n高风险=might（可能）\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音开头确实念到 Her mom is a doctor and her dad is a lawyer，但那是讲小侄女父母\n的引子，并非\"牛津研究的结论\"；选项把这两个原词搬来冒充结论，是答非所问。\n借用原文：Her mom is a doctor and her dad is a lawyer\nC) 未提及\n原文结论是\"约一半工作有被自动化的高风险\"，是个明确的比例判断，并没有说就业市场\"变\n得难以预测（unpredictable）\"；这层意思录音从未表达，属凭印象推出来的。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里 In 2013, researchers at Oxford University did a study 中的 2013 是\"做研究的年\n份\"，选项却说\"机器学习到 2013 年将具颠覆性\"，把这个年份偷换成了\"颠覆发生/达成的时\n间\"，时间含义被改了。\n借用原文：In 2013, researchers at Oxford University did a study on the future of work", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn about Kaggle company's winning programs?", "options": ["A) They are widely applicable for massive open online courses.", "B) They are now being used by numerous high school teachers.", "C) They could read as many as 10,000 essays in a single minute.", "D) They could grade high-school essays just like human teachers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They could grade high-school essays just like human teachers.\n【定位】The winning programs were able to match the grades given by human teachers.\n那些获胜的程序能够给出与人类老师所打分数相符的成绩。\n【信号】主体词 winning programs 直接出现。原文 The winning programs were able to 与题\n干主体逐字对上，主体词出现处就是答案句；这种\"题干主体在原文原样念出\"的情况，紧跟\n其后的能力描述即落点。\n【替换】match the grades given by human teachers ↔ grade high-school essays just like human teachers\n\"分数相符\"= 像老师一样评分\ngrade high-school essays ↔ grade high-school essays\n评高中作文（原任务对象）\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音谈的是批高中作文，从未提到\"massive open online courses（慕课）\"，更没说这些\n程序广泛适用于网课；这是凭\"网课也涉及作文\"的联想脑补出来的。\nB) 张冠李戴\n录音确有 human teachers 一词，但说的是\"程序的分与老师相符\"，并非\"许多高中老师在\n使用这些程序\"；选项把 teachers 从\"被对标的人\"偷换成\"使用者\"，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：match the grades given by human teachers\nC) 原词陷阱\n\"一分钟读一万篇\"是把后文 A teacher might read 10,000 essays over a 40-year career\n和 A machine can read millions of essays within minutes 的数字拼凑改造而来，且讲的\n是\"读得快/多\"，而本题问 winning programs 的本事在于\"评得准\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：A teacher might read 10,000 essays over a 40-year career", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the fundamental limitation of machine learning?", "options": ["A) It needs instructions throughout the process.", "B) It does poorly on frequent, high-volume tasks.", "C) It has to rely on huge amounts of previous data.", "D) It is slow when it comes to tracking novel things."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has to rely on huge amounts of previous data.\n【定位】The fundamental limitation of machine learning is that it needs to learn from large volumes of\npast data.\n机器学习的根本局限在于：它必须从大量的历史数据中学习。\n【信号】原词 fundamental limitation + 转折 But 后落点。本段以 But there are things we can\ndo that machines cannot 转折开场，随后 The fundamental limitation of machine\nlearning is that 与题干逐字呼应，这种\"转折后 + 原话点题\"的句子就是答案句。\n【替换】it needs to learn from large volumes of past data ↔\nIt has to rely on huge amounts of previous data\n整句几乎逐词同义替换\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音从未说机器学习\"全过程都需要人给指令\"；它强调的是\"需要从历史数据中学习\"，而\n非\"需要指令\"。throughout the process 这种绝对化说法是凭印象加上去的。\nB) 与原文相反\n录音说 We have no chance of competing against machines on frequent, high-\nvolume tasks——高频海量任务上人类根本拼不过机器，即机器在此最强；选项却说机器\n在\"frequent, high-volume tasks\"上\"做得差\"，把强项说成短板，正话反说。\n借用原文：We have no chance of competing against machines on frequent, high-volume tasks\nD) 偷换概念\n录音确实说 Where machines have made very little progress is in tackling novel\nsituations——机器在应对新情境上进展甚微，但题问的是\"根本局限\"，原文给的根本局限\n是\"必须依赖旧数据\"；而且选项说\"追踪新事物时'慢'（slow）\"，原文说的是\"进展很少/处理\n不了\"，并非\"慢\"，含义被改。\n借用原文：Where machines have made very little progress is in tackling novel situations", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What has the speaker previously talked about?", "options": ["A) The engineering problems with solar power.", "B) The generation of steam with the latest technology.", "C) The importance of exploring new energy sources.", "D) The theoretical aspects of sustainable energy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The theoretical aspects of sustainable energy.\n【定位】We've talked recently about the importance of sustainable energy. We've also talked about the\ndifferent theories on how that can be done. So far, our discussions have all been theoretical.\n我们最近谈过可持续能源的重要性。我们也谈过关于如何实现它的各种理论。到目前为止，\n我们的讨论都还停留在理论层面。\n【信号】完成时回顾句 We've talked + 收束句 all been theoretical。We've also talked about\nthe different theories 是完成时回顾，紧接着 our discussions have all been theoretical\n一句把之前内容定性为理论，这句收束语就是答案信号——它一句话概括了前情。\n【替换】the different theories on how that can be done ↔ The theoretical aspects of sustainable energy\ntheories 改写为 theoretical aspects\nthe importance of sustainable energy ↔ sustainable energy\n话题词 sustainable energy 原样对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThe engineering problems with solar power 借了录音里 one engineer 及后文工程内容\n的影子，但工程实践是说话人即将转入的 practical question，不是 previously（之前）讲\n的内容，时间点错位。\n借用原文：one engineer, Tim Castleman, believes it's possible\nB) 原词陷阱\nThe generation of steam with the latest technology 借了录音后面反复出现的 steam 和\ntechnology，但用蒸汽这套技术是说话人转向的 practical question（实践问题），属于现\n在要讲、不是之前讲过的，答非所问。\n借用原文：run a 140,000 kilogram train on just the steam generated by solar power\nC) 偷换概念\nThe importance of exploring new energy sources 看似贴主题，但录音回顾的是 the\nimportance of sustainable energy（可持续能源的重要性）和各种理论，并没有讲探索新\n能源的重要性，exploring new energy sources 是无中生有的拔高。\n借用原文：the importance of sustainable energy", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is Tim Castleman trying to do in Sacramento?", "options": ["A) Drive trains with solar energy.", "B) Upgrade the city's train facilities.", "C) Build a new ten-kilometre railway line.", "D) Cut down the city's energy consumption."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Drive trains with solar energy.\n【定位】Castleman is campaigning for a new steam train that runs without any fire and could run on\nan existing 10-kilometre line. Castleman wants to build an array of solar magnifying mirrors\nat one end of the line to collect and focus heat onto water-filled tubes. This would generate\nsteam that could be used to fill tanks on a small steam train without the use of fire.\n卡斯尔曼正在倡导一种不用任何明火、能在现有 10 公里线路上运行的新型蒸汽火车。卡斯\n尔曼想在线路一端建一组太阳能聚光镜，收集并把热量聚焦到充水的管道上。这会产生蒸\n汽，用来给一列小型蒸汽火车的水箱充气，而无需用火。\n【信号】人物意图句 Castleman wants to build... 及整套目标描述。Castleman wants to build\nan array of solar magnifying mirrors 是直接的人物意图句（wants to），加上前面 a\nnew steam train that runs without any fire，整段都在说他想用太阳能产蒸汽来跑火车，\n这就是答案信号。\n【替换】build an array of solar magnifying mirrors ↔ Drive trains with solar energy\n建聚光镜产蒸汽 = 用太阳能驱动火车\nsteam generated by solar power ↔ solar energy\nsolar power 改写为 solar energy\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nUpgrade the city's train facilities 借了录音 the upgrading of its rail yard（铁路调车场\n升级）这个词，但升级是这件事发生的背景，Castleman 本人的目标是建太阳能蒸汽火车，\n不是去升级设施，答非所问。\n借用原文：As part of the upgrading of its rail yard\nC) 与原文相反\nBuild a new ten-kilometre railway line 借了原文 ten-kilometre 这个数字，但录音说的\n是 an existing 10-kilometre line（现有的 10 公里线路），他要在现有线上跑车，不是新\n建一条线，与原文相反。\n借用原文：could run on an existing 10-kilometre line\nD) 过度推断\nCut down the city's energy consumption 沾了录音 a green alternative（绿色替代方\n案）的环保意味，但录音只说这可能给通勤者一个绿色选择，没说他的目标是削减全城能\n耗，属拔高推断。\n借用原文：offering city commuters a green alternative to their cars", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What has a Japanese research team tried to do?", "options": ["A) Build a tank for keeping calcium oxide.", "B) Find a new material for storing energy.", "C) Recover super-heated steam.", "D) Collect carbon dioxide gas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Find a new material for storing energy.\n【定位】Energy to drive a steam locomotive can be stored in other materials besides water. For\nexample, a team at Tohoku University in Japan has studied materials that can store large\namounts of heat.\n驱动蒸汽机车的能量，除了水之外还可以储存在别的材料里。例如，日本东北大学的一个团\n队研究了能储存大量热量的材料。\n【信号】总起句 stored in other materials + 例证 has studied materials。本段开头 Energy ...\ncan be stored in other materials besides water 是总起句，点明本段主题是寻找别的储能\n材料；紧接着 a team at Tohoku University in Japan has studied materials that can\nstore large amounts of heat 给出团队的具体做法，这两句合起来就是答案信号。\n【替换】stored in other materials besides water ↔ Find a new material for storing energy\n用水以外的材料储能 = 找新储能材料\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nBuild a tank for keeping calcium oxide 借了原文 calcium oxide（氧化钙）这个词，但\n录音说罐子里存的是 The gas can be stored under pressure in a tank（气体存在罐\n中），剩下的 calcium oxide 是留在原处的固体、不是被存进罐里的，对象张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The gas can be stored under pressure in a tank, leaving calcium oxide\nC) 原词陷阱\nRecover super-heated steam 借了原文末句 generate superheated steam，但那是\nValentine 说的理论上能达到的效果，且是 recover the energy 的过程，不是日本团队尝试\n做的事本身，答非所问。\n借用原文：this can create a high enough temperature to generate superheated steam\nD) 偷换概念\nCollect carbon dioxide gas 借了原文 drives off carbon dioxide gas，二氧化碳确实被存\n进罐子，但这只是名古屋团队储能流程里的一步手段，题问的是团队尝试做什么——核心是\n找钙化合物作储能材料，而非单纯收集二氧化碳，以偏概全。\n借用原文：Heating this chemical compound drives off carbon dioxide gas", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What is one cause of the current crisis in care for the elderly in England?", "options": ["A) The lack of supervision by both the national and local governments.", "B) The impact of the current economic crisis at home and abroad.", "C) The poor management of day centres and home help services.", "D) The poor relation between national health and social care services."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The poor relation between national health and social care services.\n【定位】Second, they rely on a system that has long been marked by a poor relation between national\nhealth and social care services.\n第二，他们依赖于一套长期以来以国家医疗服务与社会照护服务之间衔接不良为特征的\n体系。\n【信号】并列信号 Second 引出第二条原因。首段以 two main causes 起头，用 First 与 Second\n罗列两条原因；Second 之后这句正是其中一条成因。\n【替换】a poor relation between national health and social care services ↔\nThe poor relation between national health and social care services.\n近乎原句保留的关键表述\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借用录音里反复出现的 local governments 制造熟耳感，但录音说的两条原因是\"人活得\n更久、需求更复杂\"与\"体系衔接差\"，从未把\"国家与地方政府监管缺失\"列为危机原因。\n借用原文：supervise care homes run by independent organizations\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音第 5 段确有 a time of financial crisis，但那是讲 1980 年代资金减少的背景，不是当\n前老年护理危机的两条主因之一；B 把别处的\"经济危机\"挪来当成因。\n借用原文：at a time of financial crisis, funding diminished and little changed\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 里的 day centres and home help services 直接取自录音第 2 段 day centres and\nhome helpers，但原文只是在介绍服务种类，没说\"这些服务管理不善\"是当前危机的原\n因。\n借用原文：meals, day centres and home helpers and other subsidized services", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the speaker say about residential and community care?", "options": ["A) It was mainly provided by voluntary services.", "B) It mainly caters to the needs of the privileged.", "C) It called for a sufficient number of volunteers.", "D) It has deteriorated over the past sixty years."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It was mainly provided by voluntary services.\n【定位】No such vision guided residential and community care though. The care was substantially\nprovided by voluntary services which worked together with local authorities as they long had,\nwith eligibility based on income.\n然而，居家与社区照护却没有这样的理念指引。这种照护主要由志愿服务提供，它们一如既\n往地与地方当局协作，资格依据收入而定。\n【信号】对象名出现+核心动词 provided by。句子点名 residential and community care，紧接\n着用 substantially provided by voluntary services 说明它的供给来源，正是题目所问。\n【替换】substantially provided by voluntary services ↔ It was mainly provided by voluntary services.\nsubstantially=mainly 的同义替换\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说 eligibility based on income（按收入定资格），B 却说服务\"特权阶层\"(the\nprivileged)，方向相反——按收入往往是照顾低收入者，并非偏向特权者。\n借用原文：with eligibility based on income\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 里的 volunteers 蹭录音的 voluntary services，但原文只说服务\"由\"志愿组织提供，没有\n说\"呼吁要足够多志愿者\"(called for a sufficient number)，属凭空添加。\n借用原文：provided by voluntary services\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 deteriorated over the past sixty years 蹭了末段 60 years of criticism 里的时间跨\n度，但那是讲\"批评了 60 年\"，本题问的是这种照护的供给来源，原文没说它\"在 60 年里恶\n化\"。\n借用原文：Why has 60 years of criticism produced so little change?", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What made management of care for the elderly more difficult in the 1980s?", "options": ["A) Their longer lifespans.", "B) Fewer home helpers available.", "C) Their preference for private services.", "D) More of them suffering serious illnesses."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Their preference for private services.\n【定位】In the 1980s, the government cut spending. Meanwhile, preference for private over public\nservices made management even more difficult.\n在 1980 年代，政府削减开支。与此同时，对私营服务而非公共服务的偏好使管理变得更加\n困难。\n【信号】时间 1980s + made…difficult 直接点题。句中 In the 1980s 锁定年代，made\nmanagement even more difficult 与题干 made management…more difficult 几乎逐词\n对应，紧贴其前的 preference 即致因。\n【替换】preference for private over public services ↔ Their preference for private services.\n压缩式同义改写\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 的 longer lifespans 对应录音第 3 段 life expectancy has risen，那是讲危机的大背景，\n不是本题\"1980 年代使管理更难\"的直接原因。\n借用原文：life expectancy has risen from 66 for a male at best in 1948 to around 80 now\nB) 答非所问\n录音确有 doing little to stop the erosion of available aid 暗示援助在减少，但本题致因\n句明确指向\"偏好私营服务\"，B 把别处的\"帮手变少\"挪来顶替。\n借用原文：the erosion of available aid\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 的 serious illnesses 蹭录音 the number of sick older people grew，但那句是与致因\n并列的另一现象（病人增多），用 Simultaneously 引出，并非\"made management\ndifficult\"所修饰的原因。\n借用原文：Simultaneously, the number of sick older people grew", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker say about older people in England?", "options": ["A) They are unable to pay for health services.", "B) They have long been discriminated against.", "C) They are vulnerable to illnesses and diseases.", "D) They have contributed a great deal to society."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They have long been discriminated against.\n【定位】Discrimination against older people has a long history. Additionally, those affected by\ninadequate health and social care are too vulnerable to launch the protests that have addressed\nother forms of discrimination.\n对老年人的歧视由来已久。此外，那些受不足的医疗与社会照护影响的人太过脆弱，无法发\n起像针对其他歧视形式那样的抗议。\n【信号】结尾收束句直接定性。末段以设问 Why has 60 years of criticism produced so little\nchange? 收束，随后 Discrimination against older people has a long history. 直接对老\n人这一群体下定性，是说话人的总结表态。\n【替换】Discrimination against older people has a long history ↔\nThey have long been discriminated against.\n名词化↔主被动转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 的\"付不起医疗费\"蹭了录音 Unless you were prepared to pay 的 pay，但那是讲服务难\n获取的条件，原文没说老人\"无力支付\"，更不是结尾对群体的定性。\n借用原文：Unless you were prepared to pay\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 的 vulnerable to illnesses and diseases 蹭录音末段 too vulnerable to launch the\nprotests 的 vulnerable，但原文 vulnerable 指老人\"脆弱到无力发起抗议\"，不是\"易患\n病\"，被偷换了对象。\n借用原文：are too vulnerable to launch the protests\nD) 未提及\nD 说老人\"对社会贡献很大\"，全篇围绕照护困境与歧视，从未提到老人对社会的贡献，属无\n中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) dwells\n动词\n【词性槽】where 引导的定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】“岛狐栖息于此”最贴主语与全文话题\n主语是 a magical creature called the island fox（一只被称为岛狐的神奇生灵），与圣克\n鲁斯岛的关系是“居住、栖身”；dwells 表示“栖息、居住”，与开篇“Just off the coast of\nSouthern California sits Santa Cruz Island”所搭建的“某岛 + 某动物栖身于此”格式完全吻\n合：Just off the coast of Southern California sits Santa Cruz Island, where a magical\ncreature called the island fox __(26)__.。\n【竞争词】D) declared 时态形态不符且语义不通\ndeclared 是过去式/过去分词，无法在“岛狐 ___”这个需要三单现在时的位置出现；而且“岛狐宣\n布”也讲不通。\nI) hinders 语义反向、与全文基调矛盾\nhinders 意为“妨碍、阻碍”，需要宾语；说“岛狐妨碍”既无宾语又与全文“拯救岛狐”的正面基调\n相反。\nM) remove 形态与语义双错\nremove 是动词原形，与三单主语不一致；语义是“移除”，主语换成岛狐既不及物也不通顺。\nN) tempt 形态错且语义不沾\ntempt 同样是原形而非三单形态，且“引诱”需要宾语；用在此处既不合语法也不合语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) chaos\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 in 之后\n【依据】be in chaos 是“一片混乱”的固定搭配\nbe in chaos 表示“处于混乱状态”；与紧随其后的描述 Wild pigs attracted golden eagles\nfrom the mainland, and those flying __(28)__ crashed the fox population.（野猪招来\n金雕、金雕又压垮狐狸种群）所呈现的食物链失衡完全对应。\n【竞争词】C) configuration “构造”是中性词，且不与 be in 成搭配\nconfiguration 指“构造、配置”，与生态系统失衡的负面语境不符；be in configuration 也不是\n常见搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) predators\n名词\n【词性槽】those flying ___ 中，限定词 + 形容词修饰位之后\n【依据】predators 复指上句的 golden eagles\npredators 意为“掠食动物”，与上句 Wild pigs attracted golden eagles from the\nmainland 中的 golden eagles 构成上义词/下义词关系：金雕是空中掠食者，所以 those\nflying predators 正好回指它们，并解释它们如何 crashed the fox population（压垮狐狸\n种群）。\n【竞争词】C) configuration 形态与语义都不对\nconfiguration 是单数不可数，前面 those 要求复数；且“飞行的构造”也讲不通。\nG) extinction 抽象名词不能作 those flying 的核心\nextinction 是抽象名词“灭绝”，没有可数复数形式，与 those flying 不搭；它的位置留给 35 题\n更贴。\nJ) mammal 单数形态不符且金雕不是哺乳动物\nmammal 是单数；即便变成 mammals，金雕属鸟类不是哺乳动物，与“flying mammals”逻辑\n矛盾。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— 把 mammal 当 predators 用。上文 the fox 是哺乳动物，\n第二段也出现过 mammal，考生很容易在 those flying ___ 这里凭“熟脸”往里塞\nmammal；但金雕属鸟类、且 mammal 单数不符合 those 的复数要求。防坑：单复数 +\n上句具体形象（golden eagles）双重对照，predators 才是它的上义词。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) fierce\n形容词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 与名词 war 中间\n【依据】“激烈的战争”是固定搭配\nfierce war/fierce battle 是英语中描述“激烈、猛烈的交战”的标准搭配；与后文 complete\nwith helicopters and sharp shooters 所渲染的火力强度完全对应。\n【竞争词】A) aggressive 更偏“攻击性的姿态”，需要留给 31 题\naggressive 多形容“具侵略性、好斗的”，常修饰 species/ant/behavior；而且 31 题 the\n__(31)__ Argentine ant 那里几乎是为 aggressive 量身打造，留给后面更贴。\nO) underlying “潜在的”与“战争”语义相悖\nunderlying 表示“潜在的、根本的”，强调隐于表面之下；和 launched a war（发动战争）的“显\n性、公开行动”冲突。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— 把 aggressive 提前用掉。考生看到 a ___ war 时，\naggressive 与 fierce 都是形容词、都带攻击性，很容易把 aggressive 提前用掉；但 31 题\n的 aggressive Argentine ant 是更对位的固定形象。防坑：扫一眼后面同类空位，看形容\n词哪个有更专属的搭配，把“专属位”留给“专属词”。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) declared\n动词\n【词性槽】has + ___ war on，现在完成时谓语\n【依据】declare war on 是“向…宣战”固定搭配\n本段把保护协会清剿入侵物种的行动比作打仗，has declared war on a multitude of\ninvasive species（向众多入侵物种宣战）与上一段 launched a __(29)__ war against the\npigs 的“发动战争”形成呼应；declare war on 是英语标志性搭配，直接锁定 D。\n【竞争词】E) dedicated 搭配为 dedicate sth to doing，不接 war on\ndedicate 接介词 to 而非 on，且与 war 不构成搭配；它的真正归宿是 34 题“dedicate their\nlives to protecting it”。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) aggressive\n形容词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 与名词短语 Argentine ant 之间\n【依据】“侵略性强的阿根廷蚁”是生态学固定说法\nArgentine ant（阿根廷蚁）是世界知名的入侵性物种，英文媒体常用 aggressive\nArgentine ant 形容它强势扩张、排挤本地物种的特性；本段正在列举入侵物种 a\nmultitude of invasive species here, from sheep to plants to the __(31)__ Argentine\nant.，与 aggressive 完全对位。\n【竞争词】O) underlying “潜在的蚂蚁”不成立\nunderlying 通常修饰 cause/reason/principle 等抽象名词，表“深层的、根本的”；用来修饰具\n体的“蚂蚁”这种生物，语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) remove\n动词\n【词性槽】祈使句句首，且与并列动词 let 并列\n【依据】与“让岛屿回到原貌”逻辑同向\n后半句 and let the island go back to what it was（让岛屿回到原本的样子）暗示前半句\n的动作是“去除外来威胁”；remove the threats（清除威胁）恰好是为“恢复原貌”铺路的合\n理前提，也与上文“向入侵物种宣战”一脉相承。\n【竞争词】N) tempt “引诱威胁”逻辑相反\ntempt the threats 字面是“引诱威胁来”，与“让岛屿恢复原貌”完全相反；这是典型的“词性对、\n语义反”的陷阱。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) monitoring\n动词\n【词性槽】and 后并列动名词，与 tagging 对齐\n【依据】“监测数量”与“给狐狸打标签”同链条\ntagging the foxes（给狐狸戴标签）属于野生动物追踪的标准动作；monitoring their\nnumbers（监测狐狸种群数量）正是后续监控环节，两者并列起来构成完整的“标记+追踪”\n研究流程，并与 to ensure nothing goes wrong 的目的衔接。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) dedicated\n动词\n【词性槽】who 引导的定语从句缺谓语\n【依据】“dedicate one's life to doing”是固定搭配\ndedicate sth to doing 是英语中表示“把…献给（做）某事”的经典搭配，正好套进 have\n__(34)__ their lives to protecting it → have dedicated their lives to protecting it（把生\n命投入保护岛狐），与上文研究者长期守护的形象贴合。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) aggressive", "B) chaos", "C) configuration", "D) declared", "E) dedicated", "F) dwells", "G) extinction", "H) fierce", "I) hinders", "J) mammal", "K) monitoring", "L) predators", "M) remove", "N) tempt", "O) underlying"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) extinction\n名词\n【词性槽】in the age of mass ___ 介词短语末位\n【依据】mass extinction 是“大灭绝”固定术语\nmass extinction（大规模物种灭绝）是生态学固定术语，正好把全文“岛狐脱离濒危名单”的\n故事升华到“地球正经历第六次大灭绝”的宏大背景；与 pulling the island fox from the\nEndangered Species List 的主题首尾呼应。\n【竞争词】C) configuration “大规模构造”与主题脱节\nconfiguration 指“构造、配置”，搭配 mass 后语义生硬，且与野生动物保护毫无关联。\nJ) mammal “大规模哺乳动物”不成搭配\nmass mammal 不是英语习惯搭配；mammal 单数形态也不适合放在抽象“时代”概念里。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Steinberg argued parental sharing online can be beneficial.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Steinberg + 分享有益\n【定位】Steinberg pointed out that parental sharing on social media helps build communities, connect\nspread-out families, provide support and raise awareness around important social issues for\nwhich parents might be their children's only voice.\nSteinberg 指出，家长在社交媒体上的分享有助于建立社群、联结分散的家人、提供支持，\n并就一些重要的社会议题提升公众意识——而在这些议题上，家长可能是孩子唯一的代\n言人。\n【改写】Steinberg argued ↔ Steinberg pointed out\nargued ≈ pointed out（同义近义动词）\nparental sharing online can be beneficial ↔\nparental sharing on social media helps build communities, connect spread-out families,\nprovide support and raise awareness around important social issues\n抽象 beneficial 被展开为具体收益清单\n【辨邻】 G G 段里 Steinberg 提到的是 parents or at least physicians should be aware of\nthose potential risks——风险警告，与题干 beneficial 立场相反，故排除。\nwe did find some concerns that were troublesome\nI I 段呈现 A C.S. Mott survey found 的统计数据，是把「家长的自我报告」作为依\n据，并非 Steinberg 本人的主张，故不匹配 Steinberg argued。\nA C.S. Mott survey found among the 56 percent of mothers and 34 percent of fathers who discussed\nparenting on social media", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to an expert, when children reach school age, they can help their parents learn what can and cannot be done.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专家说「学龄孩子能帮家长学」\n【定位】We'll learn more and more what our tolerance is. We can ask our kids to help us learn as a\nsociety what's okay and what's not.\n我们会越来越清楚自己的容忍边界在哪里。我们可以请孩子来帮我们这个社会一起学习什么\n可以做、什么不可以做。\n【改写】children reach school age ↔ ages 6 to 8\nschool age 被具体年龄区间替换\nthey can help their parents learn what can and cannot be done ↔\nWe can ask our kids to help us learn as a society what's okay and what's not\n主被动转换 + 同义短语替换\n【辨邻】 C C 段说 children should have a voice about what information is shared about\nthem——是赋予孩子知情权，并未谈到「孩子反过来帮家长学」，缺核心动作。\nchildren should have a voice about what information is shared about them\nE E 段强调 The children wanted autonomy about this issue，仅是孩子的诉求陈述；\n题干要求的是「孩子能帮家长学边界」的建议，故不符。\nThe children wanted autonomy about this issue and were worried about their parents sharing\ninformation about them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One mother refrained from posting her son's photos online when she considered the matter from her son's perspective.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】母亲换位思考儿子\n【定位】But when she considered posting them online, she took the perspective of her son: How\nwould he feel if his classmates' parents saw photos of him chest-up in the bathtub?\n但当她考虑要不要把这些照片发到网上时，她从儿子的角度去想：如果同学的父母看见他在\n浴缸里的赤膊照片，他会怎么想？\n【改写】One mother refrained from posting her son's photos online ↔ I kept the pictures to myself\n正反互译：留给自己 = 不发出去\nshe considered the matter from her son's perspective ↔ she took the perspective of her son\n从儿子的视角思考——近乎逐字对应\n【辨邻】 A A 段不发是因为儿子说 Can you not? 主动请求，母亲是被请求才停下；题干要的是\n「母亲自己换位思考儿子的感受」，主动源不同，故排除。\nCan you not?\nB B 段重点是 it never occurred to me that I should ask his permission to post\nphotos of him online——觉醒；题干要的是「换位思考后克制不发」的具体行动，\n落点不同。\nit never occurred to me that I should ask his permission to post photos of him online", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "According to a study, more children than parents think there should be rules on parents' sharing.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究 + 孩子多于家长想立规矩\n【定位】They cited a study presented earlier this year of 249 pairs of parents and their children in\nwhich twice as many children as parents wanted rules on what parents could share.\n他们引用了今年早些时候发布的一项针对 249 对亲子的研究：想给「家长可以分享什么」立\n规矩的孩子人数是家长的两倍。\n【改写】According to a study ↔\nThey cited a study presented earlier this year of 249 pairs of parents and their children\nstudy 同义复现\nmore children than parents think there should be rules on parents' sharing ↔\ntwice as many children as parents wanted rules on what parents could share\n倍数关系 ⇒ 简单比较\n【辨邻】 F F 段提到 the American Academy of Pediatrics offers guidelines——是机构发布的\n指南，没有「亲子数据」。题干要的是 a study，载体对不上。\nthe American Academy of Pediatrics offers guidelines recommending that parents model\nappropriate social media use for their children", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Katlyn Burbidge had never realized she had to ask her son's approval to put his photos online.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Katlyn Burbidge + 从未意识到要征求同意\n【定位】She had been posting photos of him online without asking his permission. 'We're big\nadvocates of bodily autonomy and not forcing him to hug or kiss people unless he wants to,\nbut it never occurred to me that I should ask his permission to post photos of him online,'\nsays Burbidge.\n她一直未经儿子同意就把他的照片发到网上。Burbidge 说：我们一直坚定主张身体自主\n权，不会强迫他去拥抱或亲吻别人，但从未想过自己应该征得他的同意再发他的照片。\n【改写】Katlyn Burbidge had never realized ↔ it never occurred to me\nnever realized ≈ it never occurred to me\nshe had to ask her son's approval to put his photos online ↔\nI should ask his permission to post photos of him online\napproval ≈ permission, put ≈ post\n【辨邻】 A A 段描述儿子说 Can you not?、母亲被叫停的过程；并没有出现「从未意识到自己该\n问」的反思措辞，故不是 never realized 的源头段。\nAre you going to post that online?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "A mother decided not to post her son's photo online when he asked her not to.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】孩子开口请求 + 母亲决定不发\n【定位】She laughed and answered, 'Yes, I think I will.' What he said next stopped her. 'Can you not?'\n她笑着回答：是的，我打算发。接下来儿子说的话让她停了下来——你能不发吗？\n【改写】A mother decided not to post her son's photo online when he asked her not to ↔\nWhat he said next stopped her. 'Can you not?'\nstopped her = decided not to post；Can you not? = asked her not to\n【辨邻】 B B 段聚焦母亲事后的反思——it never occurred to me that I should ask his\npermission；儿子开口请求别发的对话 Can you not? 出现在 A 段，故 B 段不是\n源头。\nNow when I post a photo of him online, I show him the photo and get his okay\nN N 段触发是 she took the perspective of her son——自我换位；题干要的是「被儿\n子开口请求时」决定不发，触发条件不符。\nshe took the perspective of her son", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "A woman pediatrician tries to help other parents by sharing her own parenting experience.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】女儿科医生 + 分享自己的育儿经验\n【定位】says Wendy Sue Swanson, a pediatrician and executive director of digital health at Seattle\nChildren's Hospital, where she blogs about her own parenting journey to help other parents.\n西雅图儿童医院儿科医生兼数字健康执行总监 Wendy Sue Swanson 说道，她在该院的博\n客上记录自己的育儿历程，以此帮助其他家长。\n【改写】A woman pediatrician ↔ Wendy Sue Swanson, a pediatrician\nWendy 暗示性别 + pediatrician 直接对应\ntries to help other parents by sharing her own parenting experience ↔\nshe blogs about her own parenting journey to help other parents\nblog about journey = share experience\n【辨邻】 C C 段中的 Steinberg 是 a legal skills professor at the University of Florida Levin\nCollege of Law——法学教授而非儿科医生，也未提到她分享自己的育儿经历，缺两\n项关键特征。\na legal skills professor at the University of Florida Levin College of Law in Gainesville\nF F 段的 David Hill, a father of five 是男性，与题干 A woman pediatrician 性别不\n符；且 David Hill 也未明确以「分享自身经历」方式帮其他家长。\nDavid Hill, a father of five", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "There are people who decide simply not to share their children's photos online.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】决定干脆不发孩子照片\n【定位】Some parents find the best route for them is not to share at all. Bridget O'Hanlon and her\nhusband, who live in Cleveland, decided before their daughter was born that they would not\npost her photos online.\n一些家长觉得对自己最好的做法就是干脆什么都不分享。住在克利夫兰的 Bridget\nO'Hanlon 和她丈夫，在女儿出生前就决定不会把她的照片发到网上。\n【改写】There are people who decide simply not to share their children's photos online ↔\nSome parents find the best route for them is not to share at all\npeople = Some parents；simply not = not at all\n【辨邻】 B B 段强调 I show him the photo and get his okay——条件性发布；与题干 simply\nnot to share 的绝对不发立场不符。\nNow when I post a photo of him online, I show him the photo and get his okay\nN N 段触发是 she took the perspective of her son——单一场景的换位决定；并非题\n干所要的「决定干脆完全不分享」的一般化政策。\nSo I kept the pictures to myself", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Parents and physicians should realize sharing information online about children may involve risks.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】家长和医生都应意识到风险\n【定位】While we didn't want to create any unnecessary panic, we did find some concerns that were\ntroublesome, and we thought that parents or at least physicians should be aware of those\npotential risks.\n虽然我们不想制造没必要的恐慌，但我们的确发现了一些令人忧虑的问题，我们认为家长，\n或至少医生，应该意识到这些潜在风险。\n【改写】Parents and physicians should realize ↔ parents or at least physicians should be aware of\nbe aware of ≈ realize；and ≈ or at least\nsharing information online about children may involve risks ↔ those potential risks\npotential risks = may involve risks\n【辨邻】 D D 段聚焦 parents and pediatricians are increasingly aware of the importance of\nprotecting children's digital presence——是描述大家「已经在意识到」，而非提出\n「应当意识到」的提醒；缺 should realize 这一建议动作。\nparents and pediatricians are increasingly aware of the importance of protecting children's\ndigital presence\nF F 段说 He sees a role for pediatricians to talk with parents about this——侧重医\n生作为传递者；与题干「家长和医生都应当意识到」立意不同。\nHe sees a role for pediatricians to talk with parents about this", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Parents who share their parenting experiences may find themselves intruding into their children's privacy.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】分享育儿经历 = 侵犯隐私\n【定位】Steinberg and Bahareh Keith, an assistant professor of pediatrics at the University of Florida\nCollege of Medicine, say most children will likely never experience problems related to what\ntheir parents share, but a tension still exists between parents' rights to share their experiences\nand their children's rights to privacy.\nSteinberg 和佛罗里达大学医学院儿科助理教授 Bahareh Keith 表示，大多数孩子可能永远\n不会因为家长所分享的内容而真正遇到麻烦，但家长分享自身经历的权利与孩子隐私权之间\n确实存在张力。\n【改写】Parents who share their parenting experiences ↔ parents' rights to share their experiences\nshare their parenting experiences ≈ share their experiences\nmay find themselves intruding into their children's privacy ↔\na tension still exists between parents' rights to share their experiences and their children's\nrights to privacy\n两权张力 ⇒ 可能侵入对方权利\n【辨邻】 G G 段说 photos repurposed for inappropriate or illegal means, identity theft,\nembarrassment, bullying by peers or digital kidnapping——是具体风险清单；题\n干要的是「分享权与隐私权对立」的抽象关系，G 段没有这一结构。\nphotos repurposed for inappropriate or illegal means, identity theft, embarrassment, bullying by\npeers or digital kidnapping\nC C 段说 children should have a voice about what information is shared about\nthem if possible——主张孩子应有话语权；并未提出「家长分享会侵入孩子隐私」的\n因果命题，故不匹配。\nchildren should have a voice about what information is shared about them if possible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What may farmers be able to do with robots appearing on the farming scene?", "options": ["A) Upgrade farm produce", "B) Enjoy more leisure hours.", "C) Modify the genes of crops.", "D) Cut down farming costs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干What may farmers be able to do with robots appearing on the farming scene?问\n的是\"能做什么\",对应具体动作,不含infer/imply/mainly等推断或主旨信号。\n【拆句】题干主干为What may farmers be able to do,情态动词may表可能性;介词短语with\nrobots appearing on the farming scene是伴随状语,with+宾语+现在分词结构,意为\"随着\n机器人出现在农业场景中\",结构平铺直叙,无陷阱。\n【定位】Perhaps it is time for farmers to put their feet up now that robots are used to inspect crops, dig\nup weeds, and even have become shepherds, too.\n或许是时候让农民们歇歇脚了,因为机器人现在已用于检查作物、拔除杂草,甚至已变身为牧\n羊人。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文谈的是农民可歇脚,并未涉及对农产品本身的升级改造;Upgrade farm produce在文\n中找不到对应。\nB) ✓ 正确\nput their feet up ↔ Enjoy more leisure hours.\n从预判\"歇脚\"到锁定Enjoy more leisure hours.,核心在于识别put their feet up的习语\n义=休闲,正面命中。\nC) 无中生有\nP1通篇未提及基因改造;Modify the genes of crops.在文中无来源。\nD) 无中生有\n原文焦点在劳力时间和效率,而非成本节约;Cut down farming costs.虽然听上去与农业\n相关,但P1并未给出节约成本的论断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What will \"farmbots\" be expected to do?", "options": ["A) Take up many of the farmers' routines.", "B) Provide medical treatments for livestock.", "C) Lead the trend in farming the world over.", "D) Improve the quality of pastures for grazing."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干What will \"farmbots\" be expected to do?问被期待执行的具体职能,落点为客观职能\n描述,不含推断信号。\n【拆句】题干为简单将来时被动结构What will \"farmbots\" be expected to do?,be expected to\ndo意为\"被期待做\",等于原文中描述\"它将要做什么\"的句子;结构直白。\n【定位】Robots are currently under a two-year trial in Wales which will train \"farmbots\" to herd,\nmonitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze on.\n机器人目前正在威尔士进行为期两年的试验,该试验将训练\"farmbots\"放牧、监测牲畜健康\n并保证它们有充足牧草可吃。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nto herd, monitor the health of livestock, and make sure there is enough pasture for them to graze\non\n↔ Take up many of the farmers' routines.\n把放牧、监测、查牧草这一组农活上抽象为farmers' routines,选项Take up many of\nthe farmers' routines.在覆盖面与语义上都与原文匹配。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是monitor the health(监测健康状况),并未涉及medical treatments(提供医疗);\n把'监测'偷换成'治疗'夸大了farmbots能力。\nC) 过度推断\n原文只说在威尔士做两年试验,并未声称farmbots要引领世界农业;Lead the trend in\nfarming the world over.显然超出原文。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文提到make sure there is enough pasture只是三项职能之一,把它单独提出来作为\nfarmbots的总职能,以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What can robots do when equipped with high-tech sensors and complex learning algorithms?", "options": ["A) Help farmers choose the most efficient and safest passages.", "B) Help farmers simplify their farming tasks and management.", "C) Allow farmers to learn instantly what is occurring on the farm.", "D) Allow farmers to give them real-time instructions on what to do."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干What can robots do when equipped with high-tech sensors and complex\nlearning algorithms?问机器人在某条件下能做什么,问的是客观能力,不含推断信号。\n【拆句】题干为What can robots do when equipped with high-tech sensors and complex\nlearning algorithms?,when引导时间/条件状语从句,主语省略并采用过去分词equipped作\n被动,等价于when they are equipped with...;主句问\"能做什么\",直白无陷阱。\n【定位】The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages, and allow engineers and farmers\nto analyze and better optimize the attributes and tasks of the robot, as well as provide a live\nstream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms.\n机器人还会学习最高效、最安全的路径,允许工程师和农民分析并更好地优化机器人的属性和\n任务,同时还提供实时直播,反馈农场里正在发生的实况。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文是The robots also learn the most efficient and safest passages,学习路径的主\n语是机器人本身,而非帮农民选择;选项把动作主体换成farmers,主谓错位。\nB) 无中生有\n原文谈的是分析与优化机器人本身的属性和任务,并未声称简化农民的农事或管理;Help\nfarmers simplify their farming tasks and management.在文中无源头。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprovide a live stream giving real-time feedback on exactly what is happening on the farms ↔\nAllow farmers to learn instantly what is occurring on the farm.\n从预判\"实时获知\"直接锁定Allow farmers to learn instantly what is occurring on the\nfarm.,关键是识别real-time=instantly、happening=occurring这两组同义替换。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是机器人向农民提供反馈(feedback),信息流向是机器人→农民;选项却说让农民向机\n器人下达即时指令,方向被颠倒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "Why are farmers pressing for robotic farming?", "options": ["A) Farming costs are fast increasing.", "B) Robotics technology is maturing.", "C) Robotic farming is the trend.", "D) Labor shortage is worsening"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干Why are farmers pressing for robotic farming?是Why类细节题,答案是文中明文给\n出的原因,而非推断。\n【拆句】题干为Why are farmers pressing for robotic farming?,现在进行时的press for意为\"急切\n要求、力推\",问\"农民为什么力推机器人化农业\";句式简单。\n【定位】However, it is farmers that are pushing for the advancements due to ever-increasing labor\nvacancies, making it difficult to maintain large-scale operations.\n然而,正是农民们在力推这一进步,原因是劳动力空缺持续增加,使得大规模运营越来越难以\n维持。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nP10给出的原因仅是劳动力空缺,而非成本上涨;选项Farming costs are fast increasing.\n在原文无对应,属凭借'科技降低成本'常识填空。\nB) 无中生有\n原文没有说机器人技术已经成熟,只是说农民们正在推动其进步(pushing for the\nadvancements);Robotics technology is maturing.文中无支撑。\nC) 答非所问\nRobotic farming is the trend描述的是现象本身,题干问的是\"为何力推\"的原因,把趋势\n本身当作原因属答非所问。\nD) ✓ 正确\never-increasing labor vacancies ↔ Labor shortage is worsening\n从预判\"劳动力空缺日增\"直接锁定Labor shortage is worsening,靠的是\nvacancies=shortage、ever-increasing=worsening两组同义改写。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author think future farms will be like?", "options": ["A) More and more automated.", "B) More and more productive.", "C) Larger and larger in scale.", "D) Better and better in condition."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题）\n【判型】题干What does the author think future farms will be like?问作者对未来农场的预测,落\n在作者的延伸判断上,属基于尾段一步推断。\n【拆句】题干为What does the author think future farms will be like?,think后接宾语从句\"future\nfarms will be like (what)\",what是介词like的宾语前置;结构稍绕,但语义即\"作者认为未来农\n场会是怎样\"。\n【定位】Future farms will likely experience a greater deal of autonomy as robots take up more and\nmore farm work efficiently.\n未来的农场很可能将随着机器人越来越高效地承担更多农场工作,而获得更高程度的自主化。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na greater deal of autonomy ↔ More and more automated.\nrobots take up more and more farm work efficiently ↔ More and more automated.\n从预判\"更高自主化\"直接锁定More and more automated.,核心是看出\nautonomy=automated这一词性转换型同义替换。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是efficiently(更高效),与productive(产出更多)语义并不等价;六级中\nefficiency≠productivity,前者强调投入产出比,后者强调产量。\nC) 无中生有\n原文谈的是未来农场的自动化程度,并未声称未来农场规模会更大;首段虽提到Suplejack\nDowns面积广,但P11并未据此外推未来规模。\nD) 无中生有\nBetter and better in condition过于笼统模糊,原文并未对未来农场作出此种整体性状况\n评价;P11的具体落点是autonomy而非condition。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the example of climate change serve to show?", "options": ["A) The importance of climate data is increasingly recognized.", "B) Adequate government funding is vital to scientific research.", "C) Government regulation helps the public understand science.", "D) Common folks' scientific knowledge can sway policy making."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the example of climate change serve to show? 中 serve to show 把焦点放\n在例证所支撑的论点上，而不是例子本身的细节。\n【拆句】主干是 What does the example of climate change serve to show?，of climate change\n是后置定语修饰 the example，句子无陷阱，问的是这个例子被用来说明什么。\n【定位】The issue of climate change also illustrates how the public acceptance and understanding of\nscience (or the lack of it) can influence governmental decision-making with regard to\nregulation, science policy and research funding.\n气候变化这一议题也说明：公众对科学的接受与理解（或缺乏这种理解）能够影响政府在监\n管、科学政策和科研经费方面的决策。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThe importance of climate data is increasingly recognized 中「日益受到认可」在\n原文找不到对应句；原文谈的是数据传递不畅导致公众误解，而非数据本身的重要性被\n认可。\nB) 偷换概念\nP2 把研究经费列为公众理解所能影响的对象之一，Adequate government funding is\nvital to scientific research 反过来说「政府经费对科研至关重要」，主宾关系正好被\n偷换。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是公众理解科学→影响政府监管；Government regulation helps the public\nunderstand science 反过来说监管帮助公众理解科学，因果方向相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe public acceptance and understanding of science ↔ Common folks' scientific knowledge\ncan influence governmental decision-making ↔ can sway policy making\n选项 D 在主语、谓语、宾语三个位置都与定位句一一对应，是这句论点的精确同义改\n写，与预判完全吻合。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What should non-scientists do to ensure their quality of life?", "options": ["A) Seek personalized medical assistance from doctors.", "B) Acquire a basic understanding of medical science.", "C) Have their individual genome sequenced.", "D) Make informed use of animal models."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What should non-scientists do to ensure their quality of life? 问的是一个具体动作而非\n推断，属于细节题。\n【拆句】to ensure their quality of life 是不定式作目的状语，主句问的是 non-scientists 该做什\n么，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Furthermore, with continuing advances in individual genome(基因组) sequencing and the\nadvent of personalized medicine, more non-scientists will need to be comfortable analyzing\ncomplex scientific information to make decisions that directly affect their quality of life.\n此外，随着个人基因组测序的不断进步和个性化医疗的出现，更多的非科学家人士需要能够\n自如地分析复杂的科学信息，以作出直接影响自身生活质量的决定。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nSeek personalized medical assistance from doctors 把「自己分析科学信息」偷换成\n「向医生求助」，主语承担的动作变了。\nB) ✓ 正确\nbe comfortable analyzing complex scientific information ↔\nAcquire a basic understanding of medical science\nto make decisions that directly affect their quality of life ↔ to ensure their quality of life\n预判要求非科学家具备分析复杂科学信息的能力，B 用 acquire a basic understanding\nof medical science 是对该能力的最贴近的同义改写，其余三项均偏离这条能力。\nC) 答非所问\n原文中 individual genome(基因组) sequencing 是描述背景趋势的，并不是要求非科\n学家个人去做这件事；Have their individual genome sequenced 把背景趋势误读成\n个人对策。\nD) 张冠李戴\nMake informed use of animal models 里的 animal models 出自 the use of animal\nmodels，但该处是举例科学话题，不是非科学家保障生活质量的手段。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why is it important for scientists to build a good relationship with the media?", "options": ["A) It helps them to effectively popularize new scientific information.", "B) It enables the public to develop a positive attitude toward science.", "C) It helps them to establish a more positive public image.", "D) It enables them to apply their findings to public health."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why is it important for scientists to build a good relationship with the media? 直接\n问原因，原文里有现成的论述句，无需推断。\n【拆句】主干 Why is it important，后接 for sb to do 不定式逻辑主语结构，整体即问「科学家与\n媒体建立良好关系为何重要」，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Much has been written about how the relationship between scientists and the media can shape\nthe efficient transmission of scientific advances to the public.\n已有许多论述指出：科学家与媒体之间的关系能够塑造科学进展向公众传播的效率。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nefficient transmission of scientific advances to the public ↔\neffectively popularize new scientific information\n预判说「高效把科学进展传给公众」，A 把它精准地改写为 effectively popularize new\nscientific information，三层语义（高效/普及/新科学）都对齐。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说传播效率，没说让公众形成积极态度；positive attitude toward science 是从\n「高效传播」额外推出的态度结果，原文未保证。\nC) 无中生有\nestablish a more positive public image 把重点放在科学家的个人公共形象上，原文谈\n的是科学进展的传播效率，无任何关于形象塑造的论述。\nD) 张冠李戴\napply their findings to public health 把「传播给公众」曲解为「应用于公共健康」，\npublic health 在第 4 段并未出现，是把后文 human health 挪用过来制造的干扰。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say is the problem with science journalism?", "options": ["A) It is keen on transmitting sensational information.", "B) It tends to oversimplify people's health problems.", "C) It may give inaccurate or distorted information to the public.", "D) It may provide information open to different interpretations."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say is the problem 是问作者明确说过的内容，定位句即答案，归\n为细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say is the problem with science journalism? 是宾语从句嵌套，\n主句问 the problem 是什么，with science journalism 是后置定语，问「作者认为科学新\n闻业的问题是什么」，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Unfortunately, pieces of science journalism can also oversimplify and generalize their\nsubject material to the point that the basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst,\nobviously wrong.\n遗憾的是，科学新闻报道有时也会把题材过度简化和泛化，以至于其所传递的基本信息被弄\n得含糊不清，最严重时甚至明显出错。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文说「夸大基础研究对人类健康的影响」，但用的是 can be exaggerated，是一种倾\n向性描述，并不等同于 keen on transmitting sensational information 这种「热衷传\n播耸动信息」的主动定性。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 oversimplify and generalize their subject material 的宾语是「题材」，而\noversimplify people's health problems 把宾语换成「人们的健康问题」，宾语被\n偷换。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe basic information conveyed is obscured or at worst, obviously wrong ↔\ninaccurate or distorted information to the public\npieces of science journalism ↔ give inaccurate or distorted information to the public\nC 项的 inaccurate 对应 obviously wrong、distorted 对应 obscured 形成一一对应，\n且不超出定位句范围，是最贴预判的选项。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说信息是 obscured（被弄含糊）或 obviously wrong（明显出错），都是「错」\n或「糊」，并非 open to different interpretations（可作多种解读）这种「歧义」\n维度。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2018-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What should scientists do to impart their latest findings to the public more effectively?", "options": ["A) Give training to science journalists.", "B) Stimulate public interest in science.", "C) Seek timely assistance from the media.", "D) Improve their communication skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What should scientists do to impart their latest findings to the public more\neffectively? 问具体对策，原文末段有现成的归因和建议，属细节题。\n【拆句】主干 What should scientists do，后接 to impart…more effectively 不定式作目的状语，\n意为「更有效地把最新发现传达给公众」，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】We believe that at least part of the problem lies in places other than the interaction between\nscientists and members of the media, and exists because for one thing we underestimate how\ndifficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences, and for\nanother most scientists do not receive formal training in science communication.\n我们认为，问题至少有一部分不出在科学家与媒体从业者的互动上，而是出在另一些地方：\n一是我们低估了科学家面对多样化受众有效沟通的难度，二是大多数科学家没有受过正规的\n科学传播培训。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文说要培训的是科学家自己，不是科学记者；Give training to science journalists 把\n受训对象换成 journalists，与原文方向相反。\nB) 无中生有\n原文末段全文谈的是科学家自身能力问题，未涉及「激发公众兴趣」；Stimulate public\ninterest in science 在末段找不到落点。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文明确说不能把责任全推给记者一方（too often the blame for ineffective\ncommunication is placed on the side of the journalists 的语气是否定的）；Seek\ntimely assistance from the media 反而要求依赖媒体，与作者立场相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhow difficult it is for scientists to communicate effectively with a diversity of audiences ↔\nImprove their communication skills\nmost scientists do not receive formal training in science communication ↔\ncommunication skills\n末段把问题归到科学家沟通能力上，D 把这条归因反过来读成「提升沟通技能」，与预\n判完全吻合，是唯一直接回应归因句的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
